(e) The contentions that (i) the true meaning of Apex Court order in Ashish Agarwal (supra) is that the notices issued under section 148, irrespective of the Assessment Year of the unamended Act, between 1-4-2021 to 30-6-2021 are to be treated as show cause notices without being hit by limitation, if issued on or before 30-3-2021 and (ii) the defence under section 149 available to the assessee would mean that if the revenue had issued any notice under section 148 under the unamended Act during the period 1-4-2021 to 30-6-2021 pertaining to assessment year 2013-14, the same would be barred by limitation under section 149 in effect means the Civil Appeal of the revenue in Ashish Agarwal (supra) was dismissed, are completely flawed. It completely fails to appreciate that the limitation period to issuance of reopening notices under section 148 for all Assessment Years prior to assessment year 2013-14 had already expired on 31-3-2019 or earlier. The provisions of TOLA obviously could not save such a time limit and the revenue could not have validly issued reopening notices for years prior to assessment year 2013-14 on or after 1-4-2019. Therefore, the defence so expressly allowed to be taken by the Supreme Court would otherwise be unnecessary;

(f)

The submission that the Apex Court, in exercise of power under article 142 of the Constitution, has deemed the notices issued between 1-4-2021 to 30-6-2021 under section 148A(b) issued within limitation and by following the manner of computation of limitation provided in TOLA, the days from 1-4-2021 to 30-6-2021 would stand excluded and, therefore, the notices could be deemed to be issued on 31-3-2021, is found to be rather fallacious. The fallacy of this contention of revenue is conspicuous inasmuch as if the notices issued under section 148 between 1-4-2021 and 30-6-2021, which according to them, are deemed to be issued on 31-3-2021, then it is obvious that the provisions of the new reassessment law introduced by the Finance Act, 2021 cannot apply as they came into force with effect from 1-4-2021 and onwards. Ashish Agarwal (supra) in no uncertain words stated that the new provisions have to apply to all such notices. Therefore, the argument urged is completely contrary to law as well as the binding directions of the Supreme Court;

(g)

As regards reliance on Touchstone Holdings (P.) Ltd. v. ITO [2022] 142 taxmann.com 336/289 Taxman 462/[2023] 451 ITR 196 (Delhi), the Delhi High Court held that the initial notice dated 29-6-2021 issued under section 148 is within limitation. No findings on the validity or otherwise of the notice issued after May 2022 pursuant to the judgment in Ashish Agarwal (supra) is given. Moreover, in that case, petitioner did not argue that for assessment year 2013-14 the time limit would have expired even under TOLA on 31-3-2021;

(i)

The contention raised that if the notice dated 30-5-2022 under section 148A(b) is valid in terms of Apex Court order in Ashish Agarwal (supra), then the notice under section 148 cannot be issued on 31-3-2021 and respondent cannot be expected to do impossible . It has nowhere been urged by petitioner that Assessing Officer ought to complete the proceedings before the show cause notice under section 148A(b) was issued. It is the case of petitioner that the reopening notice under section 148 ought to have been issued within 6 years from the end of the assessment year 2013-14. This limitation period, as extended by TOLA, expired on 31-3-2021. However, in the present case, the reopening notice has been issued in July 2022 and, therefore, beyond the statutory time limit. In any case, the Supreme Court, while invoking powers under article 142, consciously and categorically granted liberty to assessees to raise all defences available to the assessee, including the defences under section 149. This specific and express directions cannot be set at naught. Accepting this contention of the eevenue would be a travesty of justice. [Para 37]

In the circumstances, the notice issued under section 148, impugned in this petition, for assessment year 2013-14 is issued beyond the period of limitation. [Para 38]

